<!-- ============================================================
     ClickLock macOS GUI Input Capture via Browser Kill-Loop Stealer
     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1543.001 - Persistence: Launch Agent (TA0003)
       T1489     - Service Stop / browser kill-loop coercion (TA0005/TA0040)
       T1567     - Exfiltration Over Web Service: Telegram Bot API (TA0010)
       T1056.002 - Input Capture: GUI Input Capture (TA0006)
       T1204.002 - User Execution: Malicious File / ClickFix delivery (TA0002)
     Rule IDs: 104003-104007
     Author: SecOps Engineer
     Date: 2026-07-18

     Threat Summary:

     ClickLock is a macOS infostealer delivered via ClickFix social engineering
     (victim pastes a curl | bash command into Terminal). The malware installs
     two LaunchAgents for persistence:
       - com.authirity.plist: watchdog daemon that continuously kills Safari,
         Chrome, Firefox, and other browsers in a loop, forcing the user to
         enter their login password into a fake macOS authentication dialog
         (GUI input capture / credential coercion)
       - com.chromer.plist: exfiltration agent that sends harvested Keychain
         secrets and browser credentials to an attacker-controlled Telegram bot
         via the Telegram Bot API

     The browser kill-loop (T1489) is ClickLock's novel coercion technique:
     by repeatedly killing browsers, users are presented with a fake macOS
     authentication prompt to "restore" browser access. The credential
     entered is harvested and exfiltrated rather than used for authentication.

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Wazuh FIM monitoring /Library/LaunchAgents/ and ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
         on macOS endpoints.
         Required for rule 104003 (ClickLock IOC plist filename detection).
       - macOS endpoint agent forwarding JSON process telemetry (decoded_as:json)
         with process.name, process.args, and process.parent.name fields.
         Required for rules 104004 (shell LaunchAgent write), 104005, 104006
         (browser kill-loop detection).
       - Network/firewall or DNS logs forwarded via the 103000 anchor.
         Required for rule 104007 (Telegram Bot API exfiltration detection).

     Severity tiers:
       11 - killall/pkill targeting a browser (single event; may be legitimate)
       11 - Telegram api.telegram.org connection (tune if Telegram is deployed)
       12 - Shell/curl writing a plist to /Library/LaunchAgents (ClickFix delivery)
       13 - 5+ browser kill commands within 60 seconds (kill-loop behavioral match)
       15 - Known ClickLock LaunchAgent plist filename detected (IOC exact match)

     References:
       Group-IB 2026-07-16:
         https://www.group-ib.com/blog/clicklock-stealer-macos-malware/
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,credential_access,persistence,macos,clicklock,infostealer,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       FIM: ClickLock known LaunchAgent plist filenames - IOC exact match
       ClickLock installs two LaunchAgents under /Library/LaunchAgents/ or
       ~/Library/LaunchAgents/:
         com.authirity.plist - watchdog daemon executing the browser kill-loop
         com.chromer.plist   - exfiltration agent sending credentials to Telegram
       Either filename appearing anywhere under a LaunchAgents path is a
       near-certain indicator of ClickLock infection. No legitimate macOS
       software uses these bundle identifiers. Wazuh FIM monitoring the
       LaunchAgents directories will trigger this rule on creation, modification,
       or (if monitoring is retroactive) on scan detection.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104003" level="15">
    <if_sid>550,553,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)/Library/LaunchAgents/(com\.authirity|com\.chromer)\.plist$</field>
    <description>ClickLock macOS: CRITICAL -- known ClickLock LaunchAgent plist $(file) detected on $(agent.name) -- com.authirity.plist installs browser kill-loop daemon; com.chromer.plist installs Telegram exfil agent; isolate host and initiate IR (T1543.001, T1056.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1543.001</id>
      <id>T1056.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ioc_match,persistence,launchagent,clicklock,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Process: Shell interpreter or curl writing plist to LaunchAgents
       ClickLock is delivered via ClickFix social engineering: the victim
       pastes a curl | bash command into Terminal. This shell script fetches
       and writes the LaunchAgent plists directly to /Library/LaunchAgents/.
       A shell interpreter (bash/sh/zsh) or curl executing with a
       /Library/LaunchAgents/*.plist output path is anomalous: legitimate
       macOS software installs LaunchAgents via properly signed .pkg installers
       with installer scripts, not via bare shell writes. This rule fires when
       process arguments include a LaunchAgents plist path - covering both
       curl -o /Library/LaunchAgents/com.authirity.plist ... patterns and
       bash -c '... > /Library/LaunchAgents/...' patterns.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104004" level="12">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="process.name" type="pcre2">^(bash|sh|zsh|curl|python3?|ruby|perl)$</field>
    <field name="process.args" type="pcre2">/Library/LaunchAgents/.*\.plist</field>
    <description>ClickLock macOS: $(process.name) executing with /Library/LaunchAgents plist path on $(agent.name) -- ClickFix-style LaunchAgent persistence; no legitimate installer writes LaunchAgents via bare shell; high suspicion of ClickLock or similar stealer delivery (T1543.001, T1204.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1543.001</id>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,launchagent,clickfix,clicklock,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Process: killall/pkill targeting browser processes (single event)
       ClickLock's watchdog daemon (com.authirity.plist) continuously kills
       Safari, Chrome, Firefox, and other browsers to force credential
       re-entry via a fake macOS authentication dialog. A single invocation
       of killall or pkill targeting a browser by name may occasionally
       be legitimate (e.g., IT management scripts, developer workflows).
       However, any such event should be reviewed for context, especially
       if combined with LaunchAgent indicators (rules 104003/104004), and
       rule 104006 will escalate repeated occurrences to high confidence.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104005" level="11">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="process.name" type="pcre2">^(killall|pkill)$</field>
    <field name="process.args" type="pcre2">(?i)(Safari|Google Chrome|Firefox|Chromium|Brave Browser|Opera|firefox-bin|Waterfox|Vivaldi)</field>
    <description>ClickLock macOS: $(process.name) targeting browser process on $(agent.name) -- potential ClickLock browser kill-loop; single event may be legitimate; correlate with rule 104006 for kill-loop frequency pattern and rules 104003/104004 for LaunchAgent indicators (T1489)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1489</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>impact,service_stop,browser_kill,clicklock,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Frequency: Browser kill-loop - 5+ kills within 60 seconds
       ClickLock's watchdog daemon fires repeatedly to prevent browser
       restart and maintain the fake authentication dialog. Five or more
       killall/pkill commands targeting browsers from the same source
       within 60 seconds matches the behavioral signature of the ClickLock
       kill-loop. No legitimate software kills browsers at this rate.
       If this rule fires, the host is very likely actively under ClickLock
       coercion and the user may have already entered their credentials.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104006" level="13" frequency="5" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>104005</if_matched_sid>
    <same_srcip></same_srcip>
    <description>ClickLock macOS: browser kill-loop detected on $(agent.name) -- 5+ browser kill commands in 60 seconds matches ClickLock watchdog daemon (com.authirity.plist) behavioral signature; credential coercion likely in progress; initiate IR immediately (T1489, T1056.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1489</id>
      <id>T1056.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>impact,service_stop,browser_kill,clicklock,kill_loop,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Network: Outbound connection to Telegram Bot API
       ClickLock's exfiltration agent (com.chromer.plist) sends harvested
       Keychain secrets and browser credentials to an attacker-controlled
       Telegram bot via api.telegram.org. This rule fires on any connection
       to the Telegram Bot API endpoint. In environments without Telegram
       deployed, this is high-confidence exfiltration activity. In environments
       with Telegram installed, correlate with LaunchAgent indicators (rules
       104003/104004) and browser kill-loop activity (rules 104005/104006)
       to confirm ClickLock exfiltration versus legitimate Telegram usage.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104007" level="11">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <match>api.telegram.org</match>
    <description>ClickLock macOS: outbound connection to api.telegram.org detected from $(agent.name) -- potential ClickLock credential exfiltration via Telegram Bot API; high confidence in non-Telegram environments; correlate with LaunchAgent and kill-loop rules 104003-104006 (T1567)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1567</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>exfiltration,web_service,telegram,clicklock,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
